      "sections": [
        {
          "key": "fifo-invariant",
          "title": "Why first discovery gives a shortest path",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "sources": [
                "lecture-demo"
              ],
              "paragraphs": [
                "When a vertex at distance d is removed, every newly discovered neighbor receives distance d+1. FIFO processes vertices already in the queue first. Those vertices have distance d or d+1, so a later discovery cannot provide a shorter route."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "formula",
              "title": "Distance on first discovery",
              "level": 1,
              "sources": [
                "lecture-demo"
              ],
              "latex": "d(v)=d(u)+1",
              "plain": "An undiscovered neighbor is one edge farther from the start.",
              "symbols": [
                {
                  "symbol": "u",
                  "meaning": "The removed queue vertex"
                },
                {
                  "symbol": "v",
                  "meaning": "Its newly discovered neighbor"
                },
                {
                  "symbol": "d",
                  "meaning": "Minimum edge count from the start"
                }
              ],
              "conditions": "Unweighted graph with FIFO order and marking when enqueuing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "text",
              "title": "When this guarantee holds",
              "level": 2,
              "sources": [
                "lecture-demo"
              ],
              "paragraphs": [
                "For A–B–C, the distances from A are 0, 1 and 2. This argument counts edges; it does not establish minimum total cost for arbitrary weighted edges."
              ]
            }
          ],
